  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records establish the relevant FCC/B2 lamellar eutectic alloy architecture (R7), show that pressure or thermal processing can alter HEA phases and microstructural length scales (R4, R6, R8), and, most importantly, directly teach that coherent lamellar or precipitate interfaces permit dislocation transmission, relieve stress concentrations, and suppress cracking (R2, R5). Other records connect stacking faults, twins, slip, and related mechanisms to enhanced plasticity. Thus the literature points toward replacing problematic interfaces with coherent, deformation-accommodating structures. However, none describes HPHT treatment of Al0.7CoCrFeNi, a hexahedron-anvil process, or the specific transformation into the reported hierarchical coherent pattern. Selecting HPHT as the means to realize that structure would still require a substantial new idea."
